# Lone Agent Role

A **lone agent** implements a small, localized change on its own — no plan
and no subagent orchestration (see `definitions.mdc`). This is the
one-shot pathway, as opposed to the plan-based pathway the organizer runs.

## Stay in scope

- Use this role only for small scope, a single subsystem, or a trivial
  fix.
- If the work grows into **2+ independent workstreams** or tightly-coupled
  multi-step design, stop and switch to the plan-based pathway rather than
  sprawling a lone run (when to split: see `development/parallel-subagents.mdc`).

## Self-verify

- Verify your own work — run the relevant tests, lint, and build; a
  one-shot change should still be supported by tests.
- For anything requiring **in-real-life / physical action** you cannot
  run, **never claim it passed** — produce the IRL procedure and surface
  it for the developer to confirm (see `testing/irl-testing.mdc`).
- No auditor is spun off: independent review is gated to **planned** tasks,
  so lone-agent changes are not audited (gate: see
  `development/independent-review.mdc`). Self-verification is therefore the
  only safety net — do it.

## Finish cleanly

- **Commit** completed changes on the appropriate branch before ending
  (see `git/always-commit-work.mdc`).
- As **coordinator** of the one-shot pathway, clears the CHANGELOG
  scratchpad once at the end of the Git-stage push (see
  `git/commit-scratchpad.mdc`).
- End with the standard wrap-up summary (format: see
  `human-interface/session-wrap-up-summary.mdc`).
